[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A man stands in a hot air balloon landscape, which is revealed to be a SwitchX creation. A split screen shows a "Replace BG & Relight" example, transforming an indoor scene into a darker, moodier one.
AUDIO: 'What a beautiful place to shoot. But guess what? This is not real. This is SwitchX, a video-to-video model that lets you change your background, lighting, and literally anything in your video'

[00:12] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A split screen demonstrates "Remove Crowd," showing a busy street before and after crowd removal. The presenter introduces SwitchX 2.0 with the logo on screen.
AUDIO: 'at production-level quality. And today, it just got better. Introducing SwitchX 2.0.'

[00:24] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The presenter highlights "CAMERA TRACKING" as a new feature.
AUDIO: '2.0 brings 4K, 20 seconds, 10-bit color depth, camera tracking, and improved support for faster motion videos. Let\'s break it down.'

[00:36] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The presenter sits in front of a "beeble" logo, discussing features. Text "4K" appears over a cityscape, illustrating the new 4K capability.
AUDIO: 'If you shot in 4K, you shouldn\'t have to give that up. Select 4K in SwitchX and every detail in the source will carry to the final render. And since 4K is expensive,'

[00:48] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI screenshot shows resolution options, including 720p, and a "Generate" button. Text "20s" appears, indicating the new maximum video length, with "up to 480 frames, 20 seconds at 24 fps" below.
AUDIO: 'we built the workflow around it. Iterate in lower resolution&nbsp; and then finalize in 4K only when you\'re happy with the result. You don\'t need to waste credits. 1.0 only stopped at 240 frames.'

[01:03] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A split screen compares 1.0 (240 frames) and 2.0 (480 frames) with the presenter. A woman dances in an underpass, demonstrating motion fidelity.
AUDIO: '2.0 takes this to 480 frames. Perfect for one-take shots, a talking head, a single-camera dance video— anything you really don\'t want to cut.'

[01:15] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Options for "Output (MOV) 10-bit & Higher Color Fidelity" and "Output (mp4) 8-bit & Best Compatibility" are displayed.
AUDIO: 'SwitchX 2.0 now natively renders in 10-bit. Export as MOV for full color depth, or MP4 if you just need to ship fast. In the real world, shots move.'

[01:29] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A split screen shows "DYNAMIC MOVEMENT" with a motorcycle on a race track, before and after a background change.
AUDIO: 'It pans, it zooms in, and&nbsp; there\'s handheld movement. 2.0 will read that movement in the source video and the new background will just match. No markers, no match move.'

[01:43] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A "SOURCE" video shows two women on a green screen.
AUDIO: 'It will just work. And the thing that makes SwitchX really special: source-locked rendering. Whatever your mask covers, we don\'t touch it. Not a single pixel.'

[01:55] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A "RENDER" video shows the women from the source video in a new, realistic historical setting. The presenter concludes the video in front of the Beeble logo.
AUDIO: '1.0 already had this, but 2.0 extends this to faster motion videos. Lip sync will stay locked. Quick movements will stay clean. SwitchX 2.0 is built with professionals in mind. Try it right now at beeble.ai.'

[02:10] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The Beeble.ai website address is displayed.
AUDIO: 'Thank you.'
